        <section class="hero">
            <h1>Rubaiyat Archive</h1>
            <p>A living Codex of quatrains, variants, and philosophical readings.</p>
        </section>
    </header>

    <!-- ===== MAIN ===== -->
    <main>

        <!-- Overview -->
        <section id="overview">
            <h2>Archive Overview</h2>
            <p>
                This archive collects Rubaiyat attributed to Omar Khayyam and related poets,
                with notes on authenticity, transmission, and interpretive layers.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each entry includes the original Persian text, transliteration, one or more translations,
                and commentary on themes, imagery, and epistemic horizons.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Filters -->
        <section id="filters">
            <h2>Browse by Category</h2>
            <form class="rubaiyat-filters" id="filter-form">
                <label>
                    Theme:
                    <select name="theme" id="filter-theme">
                        <option value="all">All</option>
                        <option value="wine">Wine</option>
                        <option value="fate">Fate</option>
                        <option value="time">Time</option>
                        <option value="existence">Existence</option>
                    </select>
                </label>

                <label>
                    Authenticity:
                    <select name="authenticity" id="filter-authenticity">
                        <option value="all">All</option>
                        <option value="core">Core Khayyam</option>
                        <option value="disputed">Disputed</option>
                        <option value="later">Later additions</option>
                    </select>
                </label>
            </form>
        </section>

        <!-- Archive list -->
        <section id="rubaiyat-list" aria-label="Rubaiyat entries" style="border-left-color: transparent; background: transparent; box-shadow: none; padding: 0;">
            <h2 style="padding-left: 0.5rem;">Quatrain Entries</h2>

            <!-- ===== ENTRY 1 (Core, Wine) ===== -->
            <article class="rubaiyat-entry" id="r-001" data-theme="wine" data-authenticity="core">
                <header>
                    <h3>Rubaiyat #001</h3>
                    <p class="meta">
                        <span class="authenticity">Core Khayyam</span> ·
                        <span class="theme">Wine, Existence</span>
                    </p>
                </header>

                <section class="text-original">
                    <h4>Original Persian (transliterated)</h4>
                    <p>“Gar bar dil-e-man az may-e-golgun bari hast, / Khosh-tar ze jahan-e-kohan yek nafas-ast.”</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-transliteration">
                    <h4>Transliteration</h4>
                    <p>Gar bar dil-e-man az may-e-golgun bari hast, Khosh-tar ze jahan-e-kohan yek nafas-ast.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-translations">
                    <h4>Translations</h4>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Literal / scholarly</h5>
                        <p>“If there is a trace of rose-wine upon my heart, it is sweeter than the whole ancient world — just one breath.”</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Poetic (inspired by FitzGerald)</h5>
                        <p>“One breath of ruby wine upon the soul / Outweighs the ages, makes the mortal whole.”</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="commentary">
                    <h4>Commentary</h4>
                    <p>Here, wine is not merely a drink but an epistemic key — a rupture of ordinary time that grants a taste of eternity. The “rose-wine” (golgun) evokes both sensory delight and mystical intoxication, collapsing the distance between the temporal and the eternal.</p>
                </section>
            </article>

            <!-- ===== ENTRY 2 (Disputed, Fate/Time) ===== -->
            <article class="rubaiyat-entry" id="r-002" data-theme="fate" data-authenticity="disputed">
                <header>
                    <h3>Rubaiyat #002</h3>
                    <p class="meta">
                        <span class="authenticity">Disputed</span> ·
                        <span class="theme">Fate, Time</span>
                    </p>
                </header>

                <section class="text-original">
                    <h4>Original Persian (transliterated)</h4>
                    <p>“Dar charkh ze bahr-e to nagofta, / Zin sang-e siah chand khar-ast?”</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-transliteration">
                    <h4>Transliteration</h4>
                    <p>Dar charkh ze bahr-e to nagofta, Zin sang-e siah chand khar-ast?</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-translations">
                    <h4>Translations</h4>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Literal / scholarly</h5>
                        <p>“What has the firmament ever said for your sake? How many burdens does this black stone carry?”</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Poetic (inspired by FitzGerald)</h5>
                        <p>“The sky, for all your tears, has naught to tell — / This dark millstone grinds both heaven and hell.”</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="commentary">
                    <h4>Commentary</h4>
                    <p>This quatrain is often debated. Some attribute it to Khayyam’s skeptical voice, while others place it in a later Sufi tradition. The “black stone” imagery suggests an indifferent cosmos — a theme that resonates with Khayyam’s fatalism but is expressed with a harshness unusual in his core corpus.</p>
                </section>
            </article>

            <!-- ===== ENTRY 3 (Later, Time) ===== -->
            <article class="rubaiyat-entry" id="r-003" data-theme="time" data-authenticity="later">
                <header>
                    <h3>Rubaiyat #003</h3>
                    <p class="meta">
                        <span class="authenticity">Later addition</span> ·
                        <span class="theme">Time, Transience</span>
                    </p>
                </header>

                <section class="text-original">
                    <h4>Original Persian (transliterated)</h4>
                    <p>“Dam-e az to mara chand ravad, / Tā chand ze in kuh-e-gahān bar-kesh?”</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-transliteration">
                    <h4>Transliteration</h4>
                    <p>Dam-e az to mara chand ravad, Tā chand ze in kuh-e-gahān bar-kesh?</p>
                </section>

                <section class="text-translations">
                    <h4>Translations</h4>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Literal / scholarly</h5>
                        <p>“How many breaths are still mine from you? How long must I carry this mountain of time?”</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="translation">
                        <h5>Poetic rendering</h5>
                        <p>“How many moments, love, remain for me? / This age-worn mountain crushes endlessly.”</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="commentary">
                    <h4>Commentary</h4>
                    <p>Widely considered a later interpolation, this quatrain amplifies Khayyam’s existential dread into a more theatrical lament. The “mountain of time” is a powerful metaphor, but the language lacks the precise, almost clinical irony of the authentic Khayyam.</p>
                </section>
            </article>

        </section>

        <!-- Comparative notes -->
        <section id="comparisons">
            <h2>Comparative Notes</h2>
            <p>This section collects cross-quatrain patterns: recurring metaphors, shifts in tone, and differences between manuscript traditions or translators.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Wine as epistemic rupture</strong> — In the core corpus, wine dissolves the boundaries of self and world; in later additions, it becomes a more conventional symbol of escapism.</li>
                <li><strong>Time as cyclical vs. annihilating</strong> — Authentic quatrains often treat time as a neutral, indifferent force; disputed versions tend to personify time as a destructive enemy.</li>
                <li><strong>Translator’s lens</strong> — FitzGerald’s Romantic renderings amplify the melancholy, while modern scholarly translations (e.g., Arberry) emphasize Khayyam’s rationalist skepticism.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- Technical notes -->
        <section id="technical-notes">
            <h2>Archival &amp; Versioning Notes</h2>
            <p>
                This Rubaiyat archive is maintained as Markdown source in the Buzz Codex,
                then rendered to HTML for public access via GitHub Pages.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each entry ID (e.g., <code>r-001</code>) maps to a corresponding Codex record,
                enabling cross-linking, updates, and comparative analysis.
            </p>
        </section>

    </main>

